      "briefing": {
        "paragraphs": [
          "You are the new network manager of a small college campus. Students arrive at the library in three waves. Place access points (APs) so every zone where students sit has usable Wi-Fi before they arrive.",
          "Signal strength is measured as RSSI in dBm. Closer to 0 is stronger: -50 dBm is strong, and -67 dBm is the weakest signal this game counts as covered. Distance and walls weaken the signal. Each wall costs extra decibels: drywall 3 dB, brick 8 dB, concrete 12 dB (illustrative values).",
          "One AP in the middle of this library will not cover it, because the drywall and concrete walls cut the signal. Try a different spot for each section, and watch the RSSI number on every zone.",
          "Press Space or the Pause button at any time. While paused you can still build and inspect, and the clock does not move. The first wave starts when you first resume."
        ],
        "simplification": "This is a simplified model; real networks have more effects.",
        "sourceIds": [
          "itu-p1238",
          "design-target-67",
          "wall-losses"
        ],
        "example": {
          "cols": 4,
          "rows": 3,
          "aps": [[0, 1]],
          "zone": [3, 1],
          "steps": [
            "AP at the left, zone 15 m away, no walls: 17 - 40 - 35.3 = -58.3 dBm. That is above -67, so the zone is covered.",
            "Put one concrete wall in between: -58.3 - 12 = -70.3 dBm. That is below -67, so the zone is not covered."
          ]
        }
      },
