      "briefing": {
        "paragraphs": [
          "Move-in night. Students in the dorm stream video, 5 Mbps each. Every AP can carry 100 Mbps, and the whole dorm shares one 100 Mbps uplink to the rest of the network.",
          "When a zone is unhappy for 10 seconds a trouble ticket opens. Pick the cause that the numbers support: Weak signal, AP overloaded, Channel interference, Uplink congested, or Uplink down. Each wrong answer tells you which value ruled it out.",
          "More APs add wireless capacity but not uplink capacity. Compare the signal first, then the AP, then the uplink. Upgrading an uplink to 1 Gbps costs $600.",
          "Press Space or the Pause button at any time. The first wave starts when you first resume."
        ],
        "simplification": "This is a simplified model; real networks have more effects.",
        "sourceIds": [
          "capacity-model",
          "design-target-67"
        ],
        "example": {
          "cols": 4,
          "rows": 3,
          "aps": [
            [
              0,
              1
            ]
          ],
          "zone": [
            3,
            1
          ],
          "steps": [
            "Three APs each serve 12 Video users: 12 x 5 = 60 Mbps, which is 60% of an AP's 100 Mbps. The APs are fine.",
            "All 36 users share one 100 Mbps uplink: 3 x 60 = 180 Mbps wanted, 100 Mbps available. That is 180%, so each user gets 100 / 180 = 55.6% of their demand. A fourth AP would not help; a bigger uplink does."
          ]
        }
